INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','easy','mcq','What is the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron?','["A. -70 mV","B. -55 mV","C. +30 mV","D. -90 mV"]','A','The resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is approximately -70 mV, maintained by the Na+/K+ ATPase pump and differential ion permeability. K+ leak channels predominate at rest, keeping the inside negative relative to outside.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','easy','mcq','Which ion channel opens FIRST during an action potential to cause depolarisation?','["A. Voltage-gated K+ channels","B. Voltage-gated Na+ channels","C. Ligand-gated Cl- channels","D. Ca2+ leak channels"]','B','Voltage-gated Na+ channels open first in response to membrane depolarisation reaching threshold (~-55 mV). Rapid Na+ influx drives the membrane potential toward +30 mV (depolarisation phase).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','medium','mcq','The threshold for firing an action potential in a motor neuron is typically reached when the membrane potential rises to approximately:','["A. -90 mV","B. -70 mV","C. -55 mV","D. 0 mV"]','C','The threshold for action potential generation in neurons is approximately -55 mV (about 15 mV above the resting potential of -70 mV). At this point, enough voltage-gated Na+ channels open to produce a self-regenerating depolarisation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','medium','mcq','During the absolute refractory period of a neuron, which best explains why a second action potential CANNOT be generated?','["A. All K+ channels are permanently closed","B. Voltage-gated Na+ channels are in the inactivated (h-gate closed) state","C. The membrane potential is at +30 mV","D. Acetylcholine is depleted at the synapse"]','B','During the absolute refractory period, voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated — the m-gate is open but the h-gate is closed. This prevents re-activation regardless of stimulus strength, lasting approximately 1 ms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','medium','mcq','The Na+/K+ ATPase pump transports ions in which ratio across the neuronal membrane?','["A. 2 Na+ in : 3 K+ out","B. 3 Na+ out : 2 K+ in","C. 1 Na+ out : 1 K+ in","D. 3 Na+ in : 2 K+ out"]','B','The Na+/K+ ATPase moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP hydrolysed, making it electrogenic. This maintains the ionic gradients essential for the resting membrane potential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','medium','mcq','Which of the following best describes the role of the myelin sheath in signal conduction?','["A. It increases capacitance of the axon membrane","B. It enables saltatory conduction by insulating internodal segments","C. It provides metabolic support by supplying glucose to the axon","D. It slows conduction velocity by increasing resistance"]','B','Myelin sheaths insulate axon internodal segments, forcing depolarisation to jump between Nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction). This dramatically increases conduction velocity and metabolic efficiency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','medium','mcq','During repolarisation of a neuron, which event is primarily responsible for restoring the negative membrane potential?','["A. Inactivation of K+ channels","B. Opening of voltage-gated K+ channels and efflux of K+","C. Influx of Cl- through ligand-gated channels","D. Active transport of Na+ into the cell"]','B','Repolarisation occurs when voltage-gated K+ channels (delayed rectifier) open and K+ rushes out of the cell down its electrochemical gradient, restoring the negative membrane potential. These channels open slightly after Na+ channels.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','hard','mcq','The Nernst equation for K+ at 37 degrees C with intracellular K+ = 140 mEq/L and extracellular K+ = 4 mEq/L gives an equilibrium potential (EK) of approximately:','["A. -61 mV","B. -94 mV","C. -70 mV","D. +67 mV"]','B','Using the Nernst equation: EK = (61/1) x log(4/140) = 61 x log(0.0286) = 61 x (-1.544) = approximately -94 mV. This is slightly more negative than the resting potential, which is why K+ is not in full equilibrium at rest.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','hard','mcq','Which type of ion channel is responsible for the after-hyperpolarisation (AHP) phase seen after an action potential in some neurons?','["A. Voltage-gated Na+ channels","B. Ca2+-activated K+ channels","C. NMDA receptor channels","D. HCN (Ih) channels"]','B','After-hyperpolarisation (AHP) is caused by Ca2+-activated K+ channels (BK and SK channels). Ca2+ enters during the action potential, activates these K+ channels, and the resultant K+ efflux hyperpolarises the membrane below resting potential.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Neuron Anatomy & Physiology','easy','mcq','Axons with the FASTEST conduction velocities are characterised by which of the following?','["A. Small diameter, unmyelinated","B. Large diameter, heavily myelinated","C. Small diameter, heavily myelinated","D. Large diameter, unmyelinated"]','B','Conduction velocity is proportional to axon diameter and degree of myelination. Type Aalpha (large myelinated) fibres conduct at 70-120 m/s. Myelination reduces capacitance and enables saltatory conduction, while larger diameter reduces axial resistance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','easy','mcq','Which neurotransmitter is released at the neuromuscular junction?','["A. Norepinephrine","B. Dopamine","C. Acetylcholine","D. Serotonin"]','C','Acetylcholine (ACh) is the neurotransmitter released at the neuromuscular junction. It binds to nicotinic ACh receptors on the motor end plate, causing Na+ influx and depolarisation leading to muscle contraction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','easy','mcq','An excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP) results from:','["A. Increased Cl- conductance into the postsynaptic cell","B. Increased K+ efflux from the postsynaptic cell","C. Opening of cation channels causing Na+ influx and membrane depolarisation","D. Hyperpolarisation of the postsynaptic membrane"]','C','EPSPs result from opening of non-selective cation channels (primarily Na+ influx), which partially depolarises the postsynaptic membrane. Multiple EPSPs can summate to reach the action potential threshold.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','medium','mcq','Temporal summation in neuronal signalling refers to:','["A. Addition of EPSPs from multiple synapses firing simultaneously","B. Addition of EPSPs from the same synapse firing in rapid succession","C. Inhibition of an EPSP by a simultaneously occurring IPSP","D. Spread of an action potential along the axon to multiple terminals"]','B','Temporal summation occurs when a single presynaptic neuron fires repeatedly in quick succession, and successive EPSPs add together before the membrane potential can return to resting level. Spatial summation involves multiple different synapses firing simultaneously.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','medium','mcq','The major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord that opens Cl- channels to cause IPSPs is:','["A. Glutamate","B. GABA","C. Glycine","D. Dopamine"]','C','Glycine is the predominant inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem, acting on glycine-gated Cl- channels (GlyR). GABA is the primary inhibitory transmitter in the brain. Glycine IPSPs hyperpolarise neurons by Cl- influx.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','medium','mcq','Which receptor subtype mediates the slow, modulatory actions of acetylcholine in the autonomic nervous system?','["A. Nicotinic receptors (ionotropic)","B. Muscarinic receptors (metabotropic)","C. NMDA receptors","D. AMPA receptors"]','B','Muscarinic receptors are G-protein coupled (metabotropic) receptors that mediate slow, modulatory effects of ACh in the autonomic nervous system, heart, smooth muscle, and brain. Nicotinic receptors are ionotropic and mediate fast transmission.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','medium','mcq','The NMDA receptor requires which TWO conditions for activation?','["A. Glutamate binding AND membrane depolarisation (to remove Mg2+ block)","B. GABA binding AND Cl- channel opening","C. Glycine binding AND Na+ influx","D. Dopamine binding AND cAMP increase"]','A','NMDA receptors are dual-gated channels requiring both glutamate (ligand) binding AND membrane depolarisation to expel the Mg2+ that blocks the channel at rest. This coincidence detection makes NMDA receptors crucial for LTP and memory formation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','medium','mcq','Presynaptic inhibition differs from postsynaptic inhibition in that it:','["A. Involves GABA opening Cl- channels on the postsynaptic membrane","B. Reduces the amount of neurotransmitter released from the presynaptic terminal","C. Hyperpolarises the postsynaptic neuron directly","D. Increases the threshold for action potential generation in the postsynaptic neuron"]','B','Presynaptic inhibition involves an axo-axonic synapse where an inhibitory neuron reduces Ca2+ influx into the presynaptic terminal, thereby decreasing neurotransmitter release. Postsynaptic inhibition acts directly on the postsynaptic membrane.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','hard','mcq','Long-term potentiation (LTP) at hippocampal synapses requires which sequence of molecular events?','["A. AMPA activation then NMDA unblocking then Ca2+ influx then CaMKII activation","B. GABA binding then Cl- influx then hyperpolarisation then K+ efflux","C. Dopamine binding then cAMP then PKA then CREB phosphorylation","D. Muscarinic activation then IP3 then Ca2+ release then calcineurin activation"]','A','LTP induction requires: (1) AMPA-mediated depolarisation, (2) relief of Mg2+ block from NMDA receptors, (3) Ca2+ influx through NMDA channels, (4) CaMKII activation, which phosphorylates AMPA receptors and recruits new ones.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','hard','mcq','Miniature end-plate potentials (MEPPs) occur spontaneously at the NMJ because of:','["A. Continuous firing of alpha motor neurons","B. Spontaneous fusion of individual ACh-containing vesicles with the presynaptic membrane","C. Leakage of ACh from the axon terminal cytoplasm","D. Spontaneous opening of postsynaptic nicotinic receptors without ACh"]','B','MEPPs represent spontaneous release of a single quantum (vesicle) of ACh (~10,000 molecules per vesicle). This quantal release was first described by Fatt and Katz, establishing the vesicular hypothesis of neurotransmission.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Synaptic Transmission','easy','mcq','Reuptake of serotonin from the synaptic cleft into the presynaptic neuron is targeted by which class of antidepressants?','["A. Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs)","B. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)","C. Tricyclic antidepressants (primarily)","D. Benzodiazepines"]','B','SSRIs block the serotonin transporter (SERT), preventing reuptake of serotonin. This increases serotonin availability in the synaptic cleft. Examples include fluoxetine, sertraline, and escitalopram.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','easy','mcq','The dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway carries which type of sensory information?','["A. Pain and temperature","B. Fine touch, vibration, and proprioception","C. Crude touch and pressure","D. Itch and tickle"]','B','The dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway carries fine touch, vibration sense, two-point discrimination, and proprioception. First-order neurons ascend ipsilaterally in the dorsal columns, synapse in nucleus gracilis/cuneatus, then cross in the medulla.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','easy','mcq','Pain and temperature sensations are transmitted via which spinal cord pathway?','["A. Dorsal column-medial lemniscal system","B. Corticospinal tract","C. Spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system)","D. Dorsal spinocerebellar tract"]','C','Pain and temperature are carried by the spinothalamic tract. First-order neurons enter the spinal cord, synapse in the dorsal horn, and second-order neurons immediately cross the midline in the anterior commissure before ascending to the thalamus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','medium','mcq','The gate control theory of pain suggests that pain can be inhibited by:','["A. Activating small unmyelinated C-fibres that open the gate","B. Activation of large-diameter Abeta touch fibres that close the gate via interneurons","C. Increasing substance P release in the dorsal horn","D. Blocking the descending serotoninergic pathways"]','B','Gate control theory proposes that activation of large Abeta fibres (touch) inhibits pain transmission by stimulating inhibitory interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa (lamina II) that close the gate to pain signals from C-fibres and Adelta fibres.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','medium','mcq','Referred pain from myocardial ischaemia to the left arm is best explained by:','["A. Direct nerve compression by the ischaemic myocardium","B. Convergence of visceral and somatic afferents onto the same dorsal horn neurons (convergence-projection theory)","C. Irritation of the phrenic nerve causing shoulder pain","D. Sympathetic vasoconstriction reducing arm blood flow"]','B','Referred pain occurs due to convergence: visceral afferents (from the heart, T1-T5) and somatic afferents (from the chest/arm) converge onto the same second-order neurons in the dorsal horn. The brain misattributes the pain to the somatic (arm) distribution.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','medium','mcq','Dermatomes are defined as areas of skin innervated by which structure?','["A. A single peripheral nerve","B. A single spinal nerve root","C. A single cranial nerve","D. The lateral cutaneous nerve only"]','B','A dermatome is the area of skin supplied by a single dorsal root (spinal nerve root). Knowledge of dermatomes is clinically critical for localising spinal cord lesions. Dermatomes overlap, so a single root lesion rarely causes complete anaesthesia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','medium','mcq','Which thalamic nucleus serves as the principal relay for the spinothalamic tract before cortical projection?','["A. Ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus","B. Medial geniculate nucleus","C. Lateral geniculate nucleus","D. Anterior thalamic nucleus"]','A','The VPL nucleus receives input from the spinothalamic tract and the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system for the body. It projects to primary somatosensory cortex (S1). The VPM receives facial sensation (CN V).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','medium','mcq','The homunculus in primary somatosensory cortex is characterised by which feature?','["A. Equal representation of all body parts proportional to mass","B. Disproportionately large cortical area for the hands and lips","C. The face represented inferiorly to the legs","D. A mirror-image representation on the same side as the body part"]','B','The somatosensory homunculus has disproportionately large cortical representation for areas with high tactile discrimination (hands, lips, tongue), reflecting high receptor density. Legs are medial/superior, face is lateral, and representation is contralateral.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','hard','mcq','Secondary hyperalgesia (pain at a site distant from injury) is primarily mediated by:','["A. Peripheral sensitisation of nociceptors by prostaglandins","B. Central sensitisation involving wind-up and NMDA receptor activation in the dorsal horn","C. Increased substance P in the injured tissue","D. Sympathetic vasoconstriction reducing pain threshold"]','B','Secondary hyperalgesia results from central sensitisation: repeated C-fibre firing causes wind-up with progressive amplification of dorsal horn neuron responses via NMDA receptor activation and increased AMPA/NK1 receptor expression.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','hard','mcq','A patient with a lesion in the right dorsal column at T10 would demonstrate which pattern of sensory loss?','["A. Loss of pain/temperature in the right leg below T10","B. Loss of fine touch and vibration in the right leg below T10","C. Loss of fine touch and vibration in the left leg below T10","D. Loss of pain/temperature bilaterally below T10"]','B','Dorsal columns carry ipsilateral fine touch and vibration. A right dorsal column lesion at T10 causes ipsilateral (right) loss of fine touch, vibration, and proprioception below T10. Pain and temperature (spinothalamic) would be unaffected on the right.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sensory Systems','easy','mcq','Fast pain (sharp, pricking) is carried primarily by which nerve fibre type?','["A. Adelta (type III) myelinated fibres","B. C (type IV) unmyelinated fibres","C. Abeta large myelinated fibres","D. Aalpha fibres"]','A','Fast/sharp pain is transmitted by Adelta fibres (myelinated, 2-5 micrometers, 5-30 m/s). Slow, burning, chronic pain is carried by C fibres (unmyelinated, 0.2-1.5 micrometers, 0.5-2 m/s). Abeta fibres carry touch and pressure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','easy','mcq','Upper motor neuron (UMN) lesions are characterised by which combination of signs?','["A. Flaccid paralysis, decreased tone, fasciculations, and absent reflexes","B. Spastic paralysis, increased tone, hyperreflexia, and Babinski sign","C. Flaccid paralysis, preserved reflexes, and muscle atrophy","D. Decreased tone, ataxia, and intention tremor"]','B','UMN lesions produce: spastic paralysis, hypertonia, hyperreflexia, clonus, and Babinski sign. There is no significant muscle atrophy. LMN lesions produce flaccid paralysis, hyporeflexia, fasciculations, and muscle atrophy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','easy','mcq','The corticospinal tract (pyramidal tract) decussates at which level?','["A. Internal capsule","B. Crus cerebri (cerebral peduncles)","C. Medullary pyramids (pyramidal decussation)","D. Anterior commissure of the spinal cord"]','C','Approximately 85% of corticospinal fibres decussate at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract. The remaining 15% descend ipsilaterally as the anterior corticospinal tract.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','medium','mcq','In the direct pathway of the basal ganglia, which sequence results in facilitation of voluntary movement?','["A. Striatum inhibits GPi/SNpr, which disinhibits thalamus, which activates cortex","B. Striatum activates GPe, which inhibits STN, which inhibits GPi/SNpr","C. STN activates GPi/SNpr, which inhibits thalamus, reducing cortical output","D. Striatum activates GPi/SNpr, which inhibits thalamus, reducing movement"]','A','Direct pathway: Cortex activates striatum (D1 receptors), striatum inhibits GPi/SNpr (GABAergic), GPi/SNpr disinhibits thalamus, thalamus activates cortex. Dopamine from SNpc facilitates the direct pathway via D1 receptors.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','medium','mcq','Lesion of the primary motor cortex at the area representing the hand produces:','["A. Loss of voluntary fine hand movements with UMN signs distally","B. Loss of all movement with permanent flaccid paralysis","C. Involuntary rest tremor","D. Inability to plan movement sequences without paralysis"]','A','Primary motor cortex lesions produce contralateral weakness with UMN signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia). The hand area (knob region) is most commonly affected. Cortical lesions rarely cause permanent total paralysis due to alternative motor pathways.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','medium','mcq','The lower motor neuron (LMN) — the final common pathway — is located in which structure?','["A. Primary motor cortex","B. Anterior horn of the spinal cord (alpha motor neurons)","C. Posterior horn of the spinal cord","D. Corticospinal tract fibres in the lateral white matter"]','B','The LMN consists of alpha motor neurons in the anterior horn of the spinal cord (and motor cranial nerve nuclei). All voluntary motor commands converge here before reaching the muscle via the peripheral nerve.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','medium','mcq','In Parkinson disease, loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta leads to overactivity of the indirect pathway, resulting in:','["A. Decreased inhibition of the thalamus and excess voluntary movement","B. Increased inhibition of the thalamus by GPi/SNpr, reducing voluntary movement (akinesia/bradykinesia)","C. Disinhibition of the globus pallidus externus","D. Decreased STN activity leading to flaccidity"]','B','In Parkinson disease, dopamine loss reduces D1-mediated direct pathway facilitation and D2-mediated indirect pathway inhibition, leading to increased GPi/SNpr activity, excess thalamic inhibition, and reduced cortical activation, producing bradykinesia and rigidity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','medium','mcq','The vestibulospinal tract is important for:','["A. Voluntary fine motor control of the distal limbs","B. Postural control and maintenance of upright stance through extensor muscle facilitation","C. Transmission of pain signals from the vestibular apparatus","D. Coordination of smooth pursuit eye movements"]','B','The vestibulospinal tract (from the vestibular nuclei) facilitates ipsilateral extensor muscle tone and inhibits flexors, maintaining upright posture and balance. It is a major component of the medial motor system controlling axial and proximal musculature.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','hard','mcq','Huntington disease initially destroys medium spiny neurons in the striatum projecting via the INDIRECT pathway. Which early clinical feature does this explain?','["A. Akinesia and rigidity","B. Chorea (excessive involuntary movements) due to disinhibition of thalamus","C. Resting tremor with pill-rolling character","D. Intention tremor with cerebellar signs"]','B','Early HD destroys indirect pathway striatal neurons (D2, enkephalin+). Loss of indirect pathway reduces GPi/SNpr activity, disinhibits thalamus, leading to excessive cortical activation and chorea. Later, direct pathway loss causes hypokinesia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','hard','mcq','A lesion in the right internal capsule (posterior limb) would be expected to cause:','["A. Right-sided spastic hemiplegia","B. Left-sided spastic hemiplegia and left hemisensory loss","C. Right-sided flaccid paralysis","D. Bilateral facial weakness with forehead sparing"]','B','The posterior limb of the internal capsule contains the corticospinal tract and somatosensory thalamocortical fibres. Because the tract decussates in the medulla, a right-sided lesion causes left-sided spastic hemiplegia and contralateral hemisensory loss.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Motor Systems','easy','mcq','Which descending motor tract is primarily responsible for voluntary fine motor control of the distal extremities?','["A. Rubrospinal tract","B. Vestibulospinal tract","C. Lateral corticospinal tract","D. Reticulospinal tract"]','C','The lateral corticospinal tract (crossed pyramidal tract) is the primary pathway for voluntary fine motor control of distal limb muscles. It arises primarily from the primary motor cortex (area 4) and premotor/supplementary motor cortex.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','easy','mcq','Cerebellar lesions produce deficits that are:','["A. Contralateral to the side of the lesion","B. Ipsilateral to the side of the lesion","C. Bilateral and symmetric","D. Located below the level of the lesion"]','B','Cerebellar deficits are IPSILATERAL because the cerebellum undergoes double-crossing: cerebellar efferents cross to the contralateral thalamus via the superior cerebellar peduncle, which then crosses back via the corticospinal tract.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','easy','mcq','The primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex are:','["A. Granule cells","B. Purkinje cells","C. Basket cells","D. Golgi cells"]','B','Purkinje cells are the sole output neurons of the cerebellar cortex. They project to the deep cerebellar nuclei (dentate, interposed, fastigial) and vestibular nuclei, providing inhibitory (GABAergic) input.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','medium','mcq','The vestibulocerebellum (flocculonodular lobe) is primarily responsible for:','["A. Coordination of voluntary limb movements","B. Control of eye movements and equilibrium","C. Motor planning and timing of complex sequences","D. Coordination of speech articulation"]','B','The vestibulocerebellum (archicerebellum) receives input from the vestibular system and coordinates eye movements (via flocculus) and balance/equilibrium (via nodulus). Lesions cause truncal ataxia, nystagmus, and impaired gaze-holding.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','medium','mcq','Intention tremor, dysmetria, and dysdiadochokinesia are MOST characteristic of a lesion in which part of the cerebellum?','["A. Vermis (spinocerebellum)","B. Flocculonodular lobe","C. Lateral hemispheres (cerebrocerebellum)","D. Deep fastigial nucleus"]','C','The lateral cerebellar hemispheres coordinate skilled voluntary limb movements. Lesions produce ipsilateral intention tremor, dysmetria (past-pointing), dysdiadochokinesia, and decomposition of movement. Vermis lesions cause truncal/gait ataxia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','medium','mcq','Climbing fibres, which provide powerful error-correction signals to the cerebellum, originate from which structure?','["A. Pontine nuclei","B. Inferior olivary nucleus","C. Vestibular nuclei","D. Cuneate nucleus"]','B','Climbing fibres arise exclusively from the inferior olivary nucleus (medulla). Each climbing fibre makes powerful synaptic contacts with a single Purkinje cell. They convey motor error signals and are essential for cerebellar motor learning (LTD of parallel fibre synapses).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','medium','mcq','The dentate nucleus primarily receives input from which cerebellar region and projects to which structure?','["A. Vermis then fastigial nucleus then vestibular nuclei","B. Lateral hemispheres then contralateral thalamus (VL) and red nucleus","C. Flocculonodular lobe then dentate then vestibular nuclei","D. Intermediate zone then interposed nucleus then spinal cord"]','B','The dentate nucleus receives Purkinje cell input from the lateral cerebellar hemispheres. Dentate axons exit via the superior cerebellar peduncle, decussate in the midbrain, and project to the contralateral VL thalamus and red nucleus.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','medium','mcq','Which pathway carries proprioceptive information from the ipsilateral lower limb to the cerebellum via Clarke nucleus?','["A. Clarke nucleus (T1-L2) via dorsal spinocerebellar tract via inferior cerebellar peduncle","B. Cuneate nucleus via cuneocerebellar tract via inferior cerebellar peduncle","C. Pontine nuclei via middle cerebellar peduncle","D. Inferior olivary nucleus via inferior cerebellar peduncle (climbing fibres)"]','A','The dorsal spinocerebellar tract carries proprioceptive signals from the ipsilateral lower limb. First-order neurons synapse in Clarke nucleus (T1-L2); second-order fibres ascend ipsilaterally and enter the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle as mossy fibres.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','hard','mcq','Cerebellar motor learning (movement adaptation) involves which synaptic mechanism?','["A. LTP of climbing fibre synapses onto Purkinje cells","B. Long-term depression (LTD) of parallel fibre synapses onto Purkinje cells, triggered by simultaneous climbing fibre activity","C. LTP of mossy fibre to granule cell synapses","D. LTP of Purkinje cell to deep nuclei synapses"]','B','Cerebellar learning (Marr-Albus-Ito hypothesis) involves LTD of parallel fibre synapses onto Purkinje cells when co-activated with climbing fibre (error signal) input. This reduces Purkinje cell inhibition of deep nuclei, adjusting motor output over time.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','hard','mcq','A patient with truncal ataxia, wide-based gait, and difficulty with tandem walking but NO limb dysmetria most likely has a lesion in the:','["A. Right lateral cerebellar hemisphere","B. Cerebellar vermis and midline structures","C. Left dentate nucleus","D. Superior cerebellar peduncle"]','B','The cerebellar vermis controls midline (axial) musculature and gait. Vermis lesions cause truncal ataxia and wide-based gait without limb dysmetria. Classic cause: alcoholic cerebellar degeneration preferentially affects the anterior vermis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebellum','easy','mcq','The cerebellum receives the majority of its afferent input from the contralateral cerebral cortex via which peduncle?','["A. Superior cerebellar peduncle","B. Inferior cerebellar peduncle","C. Middle cerebellar peduncle","D. Restiform body"]','C','The middle cerebellar peduncle (brachium pontis) is the largest peduncle and carries corticopontocerebellar fibres — the main input from the contralateral cerebral cortex via the pontine nuclei. The superior peduncle carries mainly efferents.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','easy','mcq','The facial nerve (CN VII) exits the brainstem at which level?','["A. Midbrain","B. Pons","C. Medulla","D. Diencephalon"]','B','CN VII (facial nerve) arises from the facial motor nucleus in the pons, at the pontomedullary junction (cerebellopontine angle). Remember: Pons = CN V, VI, VII, VIII.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','easy','mcq','Which cranial nerve controls pupillary constriction (miosis) via the parasympathetic system?','["A. CN II (optic)","B. CN III (oculomotor)","C. CN VI (abducens)","D. CN V (trigeminal)"]','B','CN III (oculomotor nerve) carries parasympathetic preganglionic fibres from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion, where postganglionic fibres innervate the sphincter pupillae (miosis) and ciliary muscle (accommodation).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','medium','mcq','Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome results from occlusion of which artery and produces which characteristic finding?','["A. Anterior spinal artery causing bilateral arm weakness","B. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) causing ipsilateral facial analgesia and contralateral body analgesia (crossed sensory deficit)","C. Basilar artery causing locked-in syndrome","D. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) causing ipsilateral hearing loss and facial palsy"]','B','Wallenberg syndrome is caused by PICA occlusion. It produces: ipsilateral facial pain/temperature loss (V spinal nucleus), ipsilateral Horner syndrome, ipsilateral limb ataxia, dysphagia/hoarseness (IX/X), and contralateral body pain/temperature loss. The crossed pattern is pathognomonic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','medium','mcq','A lesion at the level of the CN VI nucleus in the pons classically causes:','["A. Ipsilateral lateral gaze palsy (loss of ipsilateral horizontal conjugate gaze)","B. Contralateral lateral gaze palsy","C. Loss of upward gaze only","D. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia"]','A','The CN VI nucleus contains both CN VI motor neurons (innervating ipsilateral lateral rectus) AND interneurons projecting via the MLF to the contralateral CN III nucleus. A CN VI nuclear lesion therefore causes ipsilateral conjugate horizontal gaze palsy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','medium','mcq','The rule of 4 for brainstem anatomy states that cranial nerves 5-8 are found in the:','["A. Midbrain","B. Pons","C. Medulla","D. Diencephalon"]','B','Rule of 4: CN 3 and 4 exit from midbrain; CNs 5-8 exit from the pons; CNs 9-12 exit from the medulla. This helps localise brainstem lesions based on which cranial nerves are affected.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','medium','mcq','Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) results from a lesion in which structure?','["A. CN III nucleus in the midbrain","B. Medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)","C. CN VI nucleus in the pons","D. Frontal eye fields"]','B','INO is caused by MLF lesions. The MLF connects the CN VI nucleus to the contralateral CN III nucleus, coordinating horizontal conjugate gaze. MLF lesion: impaired adduction of the ipsilateral eye on contralateral gaze + nystagmus of the abducting eye. Bilateral INO is characteristic of MS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','medium','mcq','The vagus nerve (CN X) provides parasympathetic innervation to which structures?','["A. Parotid gland only","B. Heart, lungs, and abdominal viscera to the splenic flexure of the colon","C. Bladder and reproductive organs","D. Pupil and ciliary muscle"]','B','CN X provides parasympathetic innervation to the heart (slows rate), lungs (bronchoconstriction), and GI tract from oesophagus to splenic flexure of the transverse colon. Pelvic viscera are innervated by pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','hard','mcq','One-and-a-half syndrome is caused by a pontine lesion affecting which two structures simultaneously?','["A. CN III nucleus AND MLF","B. CN VI nucleus AND the ipsilateral MLF","C. CN IV nucleus AND CN VI nucleus","D. Frontal eye fields AND CN VI nucleus"]','B','One-and-a-half syndrome = ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy (the one, from CN VI nucleus lesion) + INO on contralateral gaze (the half, from ipsilateral MLF lesion). Only abduction of the contralateral eye is preserved. Caused by paramedian pontine lesions (MS, stroke).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','hard','mcq','Weber syndrome (midbrain lesion) presents with which combination of findings?','["A. Ipsilateral CN III palsy (ptosis, mydriasis, down and out eye) plus contralateral spastic hemiplegia","B. Contralateral CN III palsy plus ipsilateral hemiplegia","C. Ipsilateral CN VI palsy plus contralateral hemiplegia","D. Ipsilateral Horner syndrome plus contralateral body pain loss"]','A','Weber syndrome is caused by a ventral midbrain lesion affecting: (1) CN III fascicles causing ipsilateral CN III palsy with ptosis, mydriasis, eye deviated down and out, and (2) cerebral peduncle causing contralateral spastic hemiplegia. Classic vascular cause: PCA or basilar artery occlusion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Brainstem & Cranial Nerves','easy','mcq','Which cranial nerve is responsible for taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue?','["A. CN IX (glossopharyngeal)","B. CN X (vagus)","C. CN VII (facial nerve) via chorda tympani branch","D. CN V3 (mandibular division of trigeminal)"]','C','Taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue is carried by the chorda tympani branch of CN VII via the nucleus solitarius. CN IX carries taste from the posterior 1/3. CN V3 carries general sensation from the anterior 2/3.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','easy','mcq','Which structure in the limbic system is most critical for the formation of new declarative (explicit) memories?','["A. Amygdala","B. Hippocampus","C. Mammillary bodies","D. Anterior thalamic nuclei"]','B','The hippocampus (and adjacent entorhinal cortex) is essential for consolidating new explicit/declarative memories. Bilateral hippocampal lesions (as in patient H.M.) cause anterograde amnesia — inability to form new long-term declarative memories.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','easy','mcq','The amygdala is primarily associated with which function?','["A. Formation of procedural motor memories","B. Processing of emotional memories, especially fear","C. Regulation of circadian rhythms","D. Spatial navigation and map formation"]','B','The amygdala is critical for fear conditioning and emotional memory formation. It receives sensory input and projects to the hypothalamus, brainstem, and cortex. Bilateral amygdala lesions (Kluver-Bucy syndrome) cause emotional blunting and hypersexuality.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','medium','mcq','The Papez circuit is associated with emotion and memory. What is the correct order of structures?','["A. Hippocampus then fornix then mammillary bodies then anterior thalamus then cingulate cortex then hippocampus","B. Amygdala then hypothalamus then periaqueductal grey then frontal cortex","C. Hippocampus then septal nuclei then hypothalamus then amygdala","D. Entorhinal cortex then dentate gyrus then CA3 then CA1 then subiculum"]','A','The Papez circuit (1937): Hippocampus via fornix to mammillary bodies, then via mammillothalamic tract to anterior thalamus, then to cingulate cortex, then entorhinal cortex, then back to hippocampus. Disrupted in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (mammillary body damage).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','medium','mcq','Procedural memory (e.g., riding a bicycle) is primarily stored in which neural structures?','["A. Hippocampus and entorhinal cortex","B. Amygdala and prefrontal cortex","C. Basal ganglia (striatum) and cerebellum","D. Anterior thalamus and cingulate cortex"]','C','Procedural (implicit) memory for motor skills depends on the basal ganglia (habit learning) and cerebellum (motor adaptation). These are non-declarative memory systems preserved in amnesic patients with hippocampal damage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','medium','mcq','Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is caused by deficiency of which vitamin, and which structures are primarily damaged?','["A. Vitamin B12 deficiency damaging dorsal columns and peripheral nerves","B. Thiamine (B1) deficiency damaging mammillary bodies, periaqueductal grey, and dorsomedial thalamus","C. Pyridoxine (B6) deficiency causing peripheral neuropathy","D. Niacin (B3) deficiency causing dermatitis, diarrhoea, and dementia"]','B','Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is caused by thiamine (B1) deficiency, classically in alcoholics. Wernicke encephalopathy (acute): ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, confusion. Korsakoff psychosis (chronic): anterograde/retrograde amnesia with confabulation, from bilateral mammillary body and dorsomedial thalamic damage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','medium','mcq','Which type of memory is MOST preserved in patients with bilateral hippocampal lesions?','["A. Episodic memory for recent events","B. Semantic memory for newly learned facts","C. Procedural memory (skill learning) and priming","D. Spatial memory"]','C','Patients with bilateral hippocampal lesions lose ability to form new declarative memories but retain: procedural memory, priming effects, conditioned reflexes, and working memory. This demonstrates the anatomical separation of memory systems.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','medium','mcq','The anterior cingulate cortex is important for which function?','["A. Primary visual processing","B. Attention, emotional regulation, and conflict monitoring","C. Fine motor control of the hand","D. Primary somatosensory processing"]','B','The anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) is involved in attention, emotional regulation, pain affect, and conflict monitoring (detecting errors or conflicts between competing responses). The posterior cingulate is involved in spatial memory and self-referential processing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','hard','mcq','Long-term potentiation in the hippocampal Schaffer collateral pathway (CA3 to CA1) requires which molecular coincidence?','["A. Simultaneous GABA and glycine receptor activation","B. Simultaneous AMPA-mediated depolarisation AND NMDA receptor activation with Ca2+ influx into the postsynaptic CA1 neuron","C. Activation of muscarinic receptors and IP3-mediated Ca2+ release","D. Dopamine D1 receptor activation and cAMP-mediated CREB phosphorylation"]','B','CA3 to CA1 LTP requires high-frequency stimulation: AMPA receptor activation depolarises CA1, Mg2+ is expelled from NMDA receptor, NMDA channel opens, Ca2+ influx activates CaMKII, leading to AMPA receptor phosphorylation and insertion. NMDA is the coincidence detector.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','hard','mcq','In Alzheimer disease, which memory type is characteristically AFFECTED FIRST, consistent with early hippocampal and entorhinal cortex pathology?','["A. Procedural memory (motor skills)","B. Implicit priming effects","C. Episodic memory — particularly recent autobiographical events","D. Semantic memory for remote facts"]','C','Alzheimer disease begins in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus (Braak stages I-II), causing early episodic memory impairment — especially short-term recall of recent events. Remote memories are relatively preserved, while procedural memory is spared until late stages.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Limbic System & Memory','easy','mcq','The hypothalamus is connected to the hippocampus primarily via which pathway?','["A. Fornix","B. Anterior commissure","C. Corpus callosum","D. Medial forebrain bundle"]','A','The fornix is the major output pathway from the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies of the hypothalamus (the first step in the Papez circuit). The fornix is a C-shaped white matter bundle containing hippocampal connections.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','easy','mcq','Preganglionic neurons of the parasympathetic nervous system release which neurotransmitter?','["A. Norepinephrine","B. Acetylcholine","C. Dopamine","D. Epinephrine"]','B','ALL preganglionic neurons (both sympathetic and parasympathetic) release acetylcholine (ACh). Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons also release ACh (muscarinic receptors), while most postganglionic sympathetic neurons release norepinephrine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','easy','mcq','Stimulation of beta1-adrenergic receptors in the heart produces:','["A. Decreased heart rate and contractility","B. Increased heart rate (chronotropy) and contractility (inotropy)","C. Vasodilation","D. Bronchoconstriction"]','B','Beta1 receptors in the heart (Gs-coupled) mediate sympathetic cardiac effects: increased heart rate (chronotropy), conduction velocity (dromotropy), and contractility (inotropy). Beta1 blockers (metoprolol) are used in heart failure, hypertension, and arrhythmias.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','medium','mcq','The fight or flight response mediated by the sympathetic nervous system includes which of the following?','["A. Bradycardia, increased gut motility, miosis","B. Tachycardia, bronchodilation, decreased gut motility, mydriasis, and gluconeogenesis","C. Increased salivation, lacrimation, and micturition","D. Decreased cardiac output and peripheral vasodilation"]','B','Sympathetic activation increases: heart rate, bronchodilation (beta2), peripheral vasoconstriction (alpha1), glucose mobilisation, pupil dilation (alpha1, mydriasis), and sweating, while inhibiting GI motility/secretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','medium','mcq','The adrenal medulla is innervated by which type of nerve fibres and releases which catecholamines?','["A. Postganglionic sympathetic fibres releasing norepinephrine only","B. Preganglionic sympathetic fibres (cholinergic) releasing mostly epinephrine (80%) and norepinephrine (20%)","C. Preganglionic parasympathetic fibres releasing acetylcholine","D. Somatic motor fibres releasing dopamine"]','B','The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion innervated by preganglionic cholinergic sympathetic fibres. It secretes 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine directly into the bloodstream.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','medium','mcq','Activation of muscarinic M2 receptors in the sinoatrial (SA) node of the heart causes:','["A. Increased heart rate via cAMP elevation","B. Decreased heart rate by increasing K+ conductance (IK-ACh) and hyperpolarising SA node cells","C. Increased contractility via IP3","D. Vasodilation via nitric oxide release"]','B','Parasympathetic activation via CN X releases ACh onto SA/AV nodes. M2 receptors (Gi-coupled) decrease cAMP AND activate IK-ACh (GIRK channels), causing K+ efflux, hyperpolarisation, and reduced pacemaker firing rate (negative chronotropy).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','medium','mcq','Which receptor mediates vasodilation in skeletal muscle blood vessels during sympathetic activation?','["A. Alpha1-adrenergic receptors","B. Beta2-adrenergic receptors","C. M3 muscarinic receptors","D. Nicotinic receptors"]','B','Beta2-adrenergic receptors in skeletal muscle arterioles mediate vasodilation in response to circulating epinephrine from the adrenal medulla, ensuring increased blood flow to muscles during exercise/stress. Alpha1 receptors cause vasoconstriction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','medium','mcq','The sympathetic preganglionic neurons are located in which part of the spinal cord?','["A. Anterior horn (C1-L1)","B. Lateral horn (intermediolateral column, T1-L2)","C. Posterior horn (all levels)","D. Sacral parasympathetic nucleus (S2-S4)"]','B','Sympathetic preganglionic neurons are in the intermediolateral (IML) column of the lateral horn, from T1 to L2 (thoracolumbar outflow). Parasympathetic preganglionic neurons originate from the craniosacral outflow (CN III, VII, IX, X brainstem nuclei and S2-S4 sacral cord).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','hard','mcq','Horner syndrome with anhidrosis of the entire ipsilateral face, arm, and upper body most likely involves which segment of the sympathetic pathway?','["A. Postganglionic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion","B. Preganglionic fibres at T1 (e.g., Pancoast tumour)","C. The hypothalamus or brainstem (first-order neuron)","D. The ciliary ganglion"]','C','The sympathetic pathway is 3-neuron: (1) Hypothalamus to ipsilateral cord T1, (2) T1-L2 preganglionic to superior cervical ganglion, (3) postganglionic along carotid to eye/face. First-order (hypothalamus/brainstem) lesion causes anhidrosis of the entire ipsilateral hemibody. Postganglionic lesion causes only facial Horner without anhidrosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','hard','mcq','A patient on a non-selective beta-blocker develops severe bronchospasm. Which receptor subtype is responsible?','["A. Beta1 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle","B. Beta2 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle (normally causing bronchodilation)","C. Alpha1 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle","D. M3 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle"]','B','Beta2 receptors in bronchial smooth muscle mediate catecholamine-induced bronchodilation. Non-selective beta-blockers (propranolol) block beta2 as well as beta1, causing unopposed bronchoconstriction. Cardioselective beta1-blockers (metoprolol, bisoprolol) are preferred in asthmatic patients.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Autonomic Nervous System','easy','mcq','Rest and digest functions are mediated by which division of the autonomic nervous system?','["A. Sympathetic nervous system","B. Parasympathetic nervous system","C. Somatic nervous system","D. Enteric nervous system"]','B','The parasympathetic nervous system mediates rest and digest functions: decreased heart rate, increased GI motility and secretion, bronchoconstriction, pupil constriction (miosis), urination (detrusor contraction), and sexual arousal (erection).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','easy','mcq','REM (rapid eye movement) sleep is characterised by which EEG pattern?','["A. High-amplitude, slow delta waves (0.5-4 Hz)","B. Low-amplitude, high-frequency, desynchronised waves similar to waking","C. Sleep spindles and K-complexes","D. High-amplitude theta waves only"]','B','REM sleep EEG shows low-amplitude, high-frequency (desynchronised) activity resembling the waking EEG — hence paradoxical sleep. This occurs with vivid dreaming, REMs, atonia of postural muscles, and penile/clitoral tumescence.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','easy','mcq','In a typical night of sleep, REM sleep cycles occur approximately every:','["A. 30 minutes","B. 45 minutes","C. 90 minutes","D. 4 hours"]','C','The sleep cycle (NREM stages 1-3 followed by REM) repeats approximately every 90 minutes. About 4-6 cycles occur per night. Early cycles have more slow-wave sleep; REM periods become longer toward morning.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','medium','mcq','Which neurotransmitter system is MOST important for promoting wakefulness and is severely depleted in narcolepsy?','["A. Serotonergic neurons of the raphe nuclei","B. Histaminergic neurons of the tuberomammillary nucleus","C. Hypocretinergic (orexinergic) neurons of the lateral hypothalamus","D. Noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus"]','C','Narcolepsy is caused by loss of hypocretin/orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus (autoimmune destruction, HLA-DQB1*06:02). Orexin stabilises wakefulness. Loss leads to intrusion of REM features (cataplexy, sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations, excessive daytime sleepiness).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','medium','mcq','Slow-wave sleep (NREM stage 3) is associated with which neurophysiological changes?','["A. Increased sympathetic tone, skeletal muscle atonia, and PGO spikes","B. High-amplitude delta waves (0.5-4 Hz), growth hormone release, and reduced metabolic rate","C. Low-amplitude beta waves and active dreaming","D. Increased acetylcholine and theta rhythms in the hippocampus"]','B','SWS (N3) features: delta waves (0.5-4 Hz, >75 microvolts), high growth hormone secretion (>80% of daily GH), reduced cerebral metabolism, and lowest sympathetic tone. It is critical for declarative memory consolidation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','medium','mcq','The sleep-wake flip-flop switch involves reciprocal inhibition between which structures?','["A. Raphe nuclei and locus coeruleus (wake) vs. VLPO (sleep)","B. Suprachiasmatic nucleus vs. pineal gland","C. Reticular formation vs. hippocampus","D. Amygdala vs. thalamus"]','A','The sleep-wake flip-flop switch (Saper): VLPO (ventrolateral preoptic area) promotes sleep by inhibiting wake-promoting monoaminergic neurons (tuberomammillary, locus coeruleus, raphe, orexin). These reciprocally inhibit VLPO. Orexin stabilises the switch. Loss of orexin causes an unstable switch in narcolepsy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','medium','mcq','Which neurotransmitter, released from the raphe nuclei, promotes sleep and is precursor to melatonin?','["A. Dopamine","B. Norepinephrine","C. Serotonin","D. GABA"]','C','Serotonin from raphe nuclei promotes sleep initiation. Serotonin is converted to N-acetylserotonin and then melatonin in the pineal gland. Melatonin secretion (peak at 2-4 AM) signals darkness and promotes sleep.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','medium','mcq','In obstructive sleep apnoea, which physiological consequence drives the chronic cardiovascular complications?','["A. Excess melatonin suppressing sympathetic tone","B. Repetitive hypoxia and hypercapnia causing sympathetic activation and oxidative stress leading to hypertension","C. Slow-wave sleep excess increasing cortisol secretion","D. REM-related atonia causing myocardial oxygen demand reduction"]','B','OSA causes repetitive episodes of hypoxia/hypercapnia during sleep, triggering sympathetic surges, endothelial inflammation, and oxidative stress. Chronic sympathetic activation leads to sustained daytime hypertension, atrial fibrillation, and increased risk of stroke/MI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','hard','mcq','Light-induced resetting of the circadian clock occurs via which pathway?','["A. Classic optic nerve (CN II) via lateral geniculate to visual cortex to SCN","B. Retinohypothalamic tract via melanopsin-containing intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells to SCN","C. Melatonin from the pineal gland directly resetting the SCN","D. Trigeminal nerve via hypothalamus to SCN"]','B','Light resets the SCN via the retinohypothalamic tract: intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells (ipRGCs) containing melanopsin (sensitive to blue light, ~480 nm) project directly to the SCN, which then coordinates melatonin via the SCN-PVN-superior cervical ganglion-pineal gland pathway.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','hard','mcq','Which molecular mechanism underlies the 24-hour molecular clock in SCN neurons?','["A. CLOCK/BMAL1 transcription factors activate PERIOD (PER) and CRYPTOCHROME (CRY) genes, which feedback-inhibit CLOCK/BMAL1 (transcription-translation feedback loop)","B. Cyclic AMP oscillations driven by adenylyl cyclase activity","C. Calcium oscillations in SCN neurons driven by IP3 receptors","D. Cyclic GMP phosphodiesterase activity in photoreceptors"]','A','The molecular clock TTFL: CLOCK+BMAL1 heterodimer activates PER1/2 and CRY1/2 transcription. PER/CRY proteins form complexes and inhibit CLOCK/BMAL1, creating ~24-hour oscillations. Casein kinase-1 (CK1) phosphorylates PER, targeting it for proteasomal degradation, setting period length.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Sleep Physiology','easy','mcq','Sleep walking (somnambulism) characteristically occurs during which stage of sleep?','["A. REM sleep","B. Stage 1 NREM","C. Slow-wave sleep (NREM stage 3)","D. Sleep-onset period"]','C','Parasomnias (somnambulism, sleep terrors, confusional arousal) arise from slow-wave sleep (N3), typically in the first third of the night. The patient is partially aroused from deep sleep, may perform complex behaviours, and has no memory of the episode. REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD) occurs during REM.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','easy','mcq','The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is formed primarily by which cells?','["A. Astrocyte foot processes alone","B. Brain endothelial cells with tight junctions, supported by astrocyte foot processes and pericytes","C. Microglia surrounding cerebral capillaries","D. Choroid plexus epithelium"]','B','The BBB is formed by specialised brain microvascular endothelial cells with extensive tight junctions (claudin-5, occludin), supported by astrocyte endfeet and pericytes. The BBB restricts paracellular transport; specific carriers (GLUT1 for glucose) allow transcellular transport.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','easy','mcq','CSF is primarily produced by which structure?','["A. Arachnoid villi","B. Ependymal cells lining the cerebral aqueduct","C. Choroid plexus of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","D. Cerebral cortex via transcapillary filtration"]','C','CSF is produced primarily by the choroid plexus (500 mL/day, with 150 mL total CSF volume). The choroid plexus uses active secretion via Na+/K+/ATPase and carbonic anhydrase. Normal CSF: clear, protein <45 mg/dL, glucose 60-80% of plasma glucose, 0-5 lymphocytes/mm3.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','medium','mcq','Which arteries form the circle of Willis?','["A. Two ACAs, ACoA, two PCAs, two PCoAs, and the basilar artery","B. Anterior communicating artery, two ACAs, two ICAs, two PCoAs, two PCAs","C. MCA branches, two ACAs, two PCAs, and vertebral arteries","D. Basilar artery only with its branches"]','B','The circle of Willis: ACoA connecting two ACAs, two ICAs, two PCoAs, and two PCAs. The basilar artery gives rise to the PCAs. This allows collateral flow if one vessel is occluded.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','medium','mcq','Normal intracranial pressure (ICP) in an adult is approximately:','["A. 0-5 mmHg","B. 7-15 mmHg","C. 20-30 mmHg","D. 40-50 mmHg"]','B','Normal ICP is 7-15 mmHg (80-180 mmH2O on LP manometry). ICP >20 mmHg is considered raised. The Monro-Kellie doctrine states that brain + blood + CSF volumes are constant; increase in one must be compensated by decrease in another or ICP rises.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','medium','mcq','CSF exits the fourth ventricle via which foramina?','["A. Foramen of Monro","B. Foramina of Luschka (lateral) and foramen of Magendie (medial)","C. Arachnoid villi directly","D. Interventricular foramina only"]','B','CSF exits the fourth ventricle via the two lateral foramina of Luschka and the median foramen of Magendie into the subarachnoid space. It then circulates before being absorbed at the arachnoid villi/granulations into the dural venous sinuses.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','medium','mcq','Cerebral autoregulation maintains constant cerebral blood flow over which range of mean arterial pressures?','["A. 10-40 mmHg","B. 50-150 mmHg","C. 100-200 mmHg","D. 160-220 mmHg"]','B','Cerebral autoregulation maintains CBF (~50 mL/100g/min) constant across MAP 50-150 mmHg. Below 50 mmHg, CBF falls (ischaemia); above 150 mmHg, forced vasodilation and breakthrough oedema (hypertensive encephalopathy). Autoregulation is impaired after brain injury.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','medium','mcq','Which stimulus is the most potent vasodilator of cerebral blood vessels?','["A. Oxygen","B. Carbon dioxide (hypercapnia)","C. Nitric oxide","D. Adenosine"]','B','CO2/H+ is the most potent regulator of CBF: hypercapnia causes cerebral vasodilation and increased CBF. Hypocapnia (hyperventilation) causes vasoconstriction and decreased CBF (used acutely to treat raised ICP). PaCO2 change of 1 mmHg changes CBF by approximately 3%.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','hard','mcq','Cushing triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) in raised ICP is best explained by which mechanism?','["A. Vagal activation from cerebral oedema compressing the dorsal motor nucleus of CN X","B. Brainstem ischaemia triggering sympathetic surge raising MAP to maintain CPP, with compensatory baroreceptor-mediated bradycardia","C. Hyperthermia from hypothalamic compression causing autonomic instability","D. Cerebral autoregulation failure causing reactive hyperaemia and bradycardia"]','B','Cushing triad: Severe raised ICP reduces CPP (CPP = MAP - ICP), causing brainstem ischaemia and a massive sympathetic surge producing extreme hypertension. Baroreceptors detect hypertension and trigger compensatory vagal bradycardia. Irregular breathing reflects medullary respiratory centre ischaemia. This is a pre-terminal sign.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','hard','mcq','Non-communicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus caused by aqueductal stenosis is characterised by which pattern?','["A. Impaired absorption at arachnoid villi with all ventricles enlarged post-meningitis","B. Obstruction at the cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius causing enlarged lateral and 3rd ventricles with normal 4th ventricle","C. Sagittal sinus thrombosis with all ventricles enlarged","D. Choroid plexus papilloma causing CSF overproduction"]','B','Non-communicating/obstructive hydrocephalus: obstruction within the ventricular system causes upstream ventricular enlargement. Aqueduct of Sylvius stenosis produces enlarged lateral and 3rd ventricles with normal 4th ventricle. Communicating hydrocephalus = impaired absorption at arachnoid granulations (post-meningitis, post-SAH).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','CNS & Neurology','Anatomy & Physiology','Cerebral Circulation & CSF','easy','mcq','Occlusion of the dominant middle cerebral artery (MCA) at its origin would produce which classic syndrome?','["A. Contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss with bladder dysfunction","B. Contralateral face and arm weakness/sensory loss, aphasia (dominant hemisphere), and contralateral homonymous hemianopia","C. Ipsilateral blindness and contralateral hemiplegia","D. Bilateral leg weakness with bladder dysfunction"]','B','MCA stroke (dominant hemisphere): contralateral face/arm greater than leg motor and sensory deficit, Broca or Wernicke or global aphasia depending on extent, and homonymous hemianopia from optic radiation involvement. ACA stroke causes contralateral leg greater than arm weakness.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed');